{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Like cedars beside waters.</b> In Sanhedrin (105b) they said: the curse of Ahijah the Shilonite was better than the blessings of Balaam. Ahijah cursed them with a reed, as it says (I Kings 14:15): ״וְהִכָּה ה׳ אֶת יִשְׂרָאֵל כַּאֲשֶׁר יָנוּד הַקָּנֶה בַּמַּיִם״ — just as a reed does not stand except in a place of water, and its stalk is replaced, and its roots are many, etc.; but Balaam blessed them with a cedar, which does not stand in a place of water, etc. And Rashi explained what is written here, ״כַּאֲרָזִים עֲלֵי מָיִם,״ as: this verse says thus — and his explanation is forced. And it appears that this is its meaning: for from Balaam's blessings we learn what was in his heart to curse them, and surely it was in his mind to liken them to an ordinary cedar that does not stand in a place of water, in order to subject them to the evil eye — like an ordinary cedar, which because it is tall everyone gazes at it; so too Israel, by virtue of being exalted and lifted up and very lofty, the nations would fix their eyes upon them. But HaShem turned the curse and put a hook into his mouth, saying: say ״כַּאֲרָזִים עֲלֵי מָיִם״ — the very document and its refutation together, for the waters shield it, for most of its base is covered by the water and it does not appear so tall, and the waters save it from the evil eye like fish. Nevertheless, the image of the reed is better than it, for it points to humility, for it is submissive before the wind, and this is its remedy. And therefore our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Taanit 20a) that the reed merited to be taken as a quill to write with it a scroll of the Torah — and as a lesson they placed in their hearts that the Torah is fulfilled only among those of lowly spirit like a reed, and not in one who is hard as a cedar. And nevertheless the cedar that stands upon water somewhat resembles a reed, from the side of its being partly covered, for this too points somewhat to humility. Therefore he said immediately: ״יִזַּל מַיִם מִדָּלְיָו״ — from the branches that are humble by nature, the waters of Torah shall flow." "And when Balaam saw that he could do nothing against them from any side, he admitted and said that he would not be able to do good or evil, etc. For the evil means to curse, and the good to bless them with a loud voice so that a curse would be reckoned to them — and this good is inferior to outright evil, because his mouth and his heart are not equal. Therefore he placed the good before the evil, in the manner of 'not only this but also that.' And after all the prophecies he said, ״הִנְנִי הוֹלֵךְ לְעַמִּי״ — the meaning is not the departure of prophecy, for he is still prophesying and going; rather, he said: here I am, going to prophesy what this people will do to my people. Therefore, ״לְכָה אִיעָצְךָ״ etc. — for this is the counsel how you will be delivered from them." "And it is close to hearing that he said to him: I will counsel you to sit and do nothing, since peace will be in your days — what is it to you to worry about the future? For ״אֵת אֲשֶׁר יַעֲשֶׂה הָעָם הַזֶּה לְעַמְּךָ״ will be ״בְּאַחֲרִית הַיָּמִים,״ and only peace will be in your days. Therefore be careful and be still. And the essence of the counsel is hinted in the word ״לְכָה,״ meaning: now come and depart from this path. And what is hinted in this ״לְכָה״ is ״אִיעָצְךָ״ — that for the time being you should go your way and let the matter be, for there is yet a vision for the appointed time."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {0 "The Aramaic phrase שְׁכִינָה דְּקָאָמְרָא הָכֵי is rendered literally as 'this verse says thus,' following Rashi's style of citing the verse under discussion."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:43:11.393349458Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}